\section{How a cost is matched to an asset}{

\code{\link[=pm_backtest]{pm_backtest()}} resolves the schedule against the column names of the return
matrix, in this order:
\enumerate{
\item an exact match on the column name, so \code{US_equity = 0.0004} prices one
series on its own;
\item a match on a trailing, underscore-delimited token, case-insensitively, so
\code{equity} prices \code{US_equity}, \code{EU_equity} and the rest, and \code{money_market}
prices \code{US_money_market};
\item the \code{.default} element.
}

Where several names match, the longest wins, so \code{money_market} beats \code{market}.
The class-level universe of the simulated market is named
\verb{<REGION>_<asset_class>} and resolves entirely at step 2. The instrument-level
universe is named \code{US_EQU_01} and does \strong{not}: build a per-asset vector from
\link{sim_assets} first, as shown in the examples.
}

\section{The ordering, and where it comes from}{

Government bonds are the cheapest thing in the book, money market cheaper
still; commodity futures are cheap; cash equities sit in the middle; corporate
credit is the dearest by a wide margin because it trades over the counter in
size, not on an exchange.
}

